WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.740
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.740
Today we are going to end the rule of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:12.060
ugly, blurry photos with a new&nbsp;
AI technique developed by Google.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:18.900
You see, most smartphones today have multiple&nbsp;
cameras, and can take these beautiful portrait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:24.900
mode photos where the subject is in focus,&nbsp;
and the rest of the image is blurred out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:32.460
This is all well and good if we have shot the&nbsp;
image perfectly, and we have in focus exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:38.100
the things that we want before pressing the&nbsp;
button. If we missed our chance, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:45.660
that’s it. Refocusing these images would&nbsp;
be amazing, if we could do it. So, can we?

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:54.780
Well, unfortunately we can. Now I hear you asking&nbsp;
Doc, why do you say, unfortunately we can? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:02.340
we have to use light fields to properly refocus&nbsp;
an image. This unfortunately requires an expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:09.420
and inconvenient array of cameras to capture all&nbsp;
the required information in and out of focus.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:16.320
Or, we can try focus stacking. That is,&nbsp;
capturing a series of images with different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.740
focus points. Focusing on different things&nbsp;
in different photos. This is not expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.320
but unfortunately, it is time&nbsp;
consuming, and not trivial to perform.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.980
But, wait a minute. I just said at the start&nbsp;
of the video that most smartphone brands today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:41.100
have multiple cameras. That typically&nbsp;
means a wide and an ultrawide lens.&nbsp;

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:47.040
If we take a photo with both of these&nbsp;
lenses at the same time, we get two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.100
images with different parts of the real scene&nbsp;
in focus. If you think that sounds reassuring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:59.940
it gets worse, look. The two lenses give quite&nbsp;
different results. The colors are different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:06.240
the focus is different. Hmm. Perhaps a&nbsp;
learning-based algorithm could stitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.660
these two together into an image that we&nbsp;
can refocus, but it looks quite difficult.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:19.500
And today, Fellow Scholars, fortunately, that&nbsp;
is a possibility. This is the result from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:27.720
work named DRBNet from just one year ago. This&nbsp;
is supposed to deblur the whole image and give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:36.120
us a sharp result. Well, it could have been a&nbsp;
lot better, look here. Not too great. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:42.540
hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars, and have&nbsp;
a look at the new technique. So much better.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:48.960
And when we try the old technique for&nbsp;
refocusing an image, we get this. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:56.160
with the new technique, we get this instead. That&nbsp;
is a huge difference just one more paper down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.480
line, especially that the previous&nbsp;
work was published just a year ago.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:07.800
And with this new work, we can now refocus all&nbsp;
of these beautiful images. And even though none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:14.100
of them are as perfect as having a real camera&nbsp;
focusing at all the objects, I find these results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:21.900
to be absolutely incredible. Not perfect by any&nbsp;
means, but remember, the two lenses are really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:28.140
different. This technique is like a classic music&nbsp;
conductor having two different orchestras playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:34.740
together in harmony. Neither of the orchestras&nbsp;
are perfect by themselves because they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:41.460
both missing some instruments, but together, if&nbsp;
directed well, they can play the perfect symphony.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:47.160
You can now take in this symphony, not&nbsp;
with your ears, but with your eyes.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:54.600
Now two more things that I absolutely loved&nbsp;
about this paper. One is that it describes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:59.640
method that is not specific to&nbsp;
a particular device. And two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.980
these ultrawide lenses are often of lower&nbsp;
quality than the other lenses in smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:11.940
cameras and are typically underused&nbsp;
and overlooked. But not here. Love it.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.500
So, from now on, unusable&nbsp;
blurry images of important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.880
family moments shall be banished&nbsp;
forever. What a time to be alive!

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.260
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

